Z||000000||HummelHund||05-03-2001||04:44 PM||||Lately my Fiero ('84 SE) has been quitting just before I come to a stop, and will only restart if I floor the gas pedal. It only does this occasionally, but it happens almost every time I take it out, so I can't drive it around very much. The very helpful PBJ said he thought this was a sign of too much fuel at idle and a bad injector. Is this the most likely suspect? I really need as many opinions as possible before I go out and spend $60 on a new fuel injector. I have replaced the IAC, MAP sensor, and all the vaccuum lines. Could it be the fuel pump, or maybe the TPS? Also, after searching the archives I saw that others who had had this problem blamed the ignition module. I had this tested at a local Auto Zone and it tested fine. Can a module test fine but be bad anyway? I really want to get my car on the road, but I think we angered the Fiero gods by buying a Volvo for my mother.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>Thanks for everyone's help!<BR><P>------------------<BR>Sean W.<BR>'84 SE 2M4 WS6 (restoration project to become daily driver)<BR>'84 Sport Coupe ($150 running car)||||reg||1
Z||000001||HummelHund||05-03-2001||07:58 PM||||One other thing I noticed today. There is oily fluid (oil, I suppose) in the bottom of my air cleaner. What could be causing this? My car needs a valve cover gasket, is this the problem?<BR>Thanks<BR><P>------------------<BR>Sean W.<BR>'84 SE 2M4 WS6 (restoration project to become daily driver)<BR>'84 Sport Coupe ($150 running car)||||reg||1
Z||000002||Wolfhound||05-03-2001||08:12 PM||||Check for codes, sounds like the oxygen sensor.||||reg||1
Z||000003||PBJ||05-03-2001||08:27 PM||||My thoughts.  I would recommend that, before you buy and more parts. Spend the money on getting it looked at by a GM dealership.  The "Iron Duke" is a great mtr, but it was designed pre fuel injection-what I am saying is the sensors, ecm and fuel injection is adapted to work on the 2.5, for this reason the 2.5 is, if not one of the hardest engines to diagnose,  find a GM dealership that has been around in the mid 80's and a tech that has experience.  Any normal engine breaks and stays broken until it is fixed, but the iron duke will start again on its own (for a while).   I am a firm believer in searching the archives but, YOUR $60.00  might be better spent at a GM dealership.<BR>-best of luck||||reg||1
Z||000004||HummelHund||05-03-2001||08:42 PM||||The only code I had been getting is the map sensor code, but now I don't get that since I replaced all the gaskets and o rings on my TBI. Could the O2 be bad but not give a code? I haven't replaced it yet, I don't know how old it is.<BR><P>------------------<BR>Sean W.<BR>'84 SE 2M4 WS6 (restoration project to become daily driver)<BR>'84 Sport Coupe ($150 running car)||||reg||1
